<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Turco-Egyptian Sudan</h1><p class="st-meta">1821 CE – 1885 CE · capital Khartoum</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Turkiyya, Egyptian rule over Sudan from 1821, which created Sudan's borders and capital before collapsing in the Mahdist revolt.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1820 to 1821 armies sent by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/egypt-under-muhammad-ali">Egypt under Muhammad Ali</a>, led by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-kamil-pasha">Ismail Pasha</a>, conquered the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/funj-sultanate">Funj Sultanate</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kordofan">Kordofan</a>, seeking gold and slaves for the army. Ismail was killed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaalin">Ja'ali</a> chief at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shendi">Shendi</a> in 1822, and harsh reprisals followed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a> became the capital in the 1820s.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Governors-general ruled through Turkish, Egyptian and European officials, imposing heavy taxes. Merchants opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Nile</a> and the south to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trades</a>, with private armies such as that of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zubayrs-bahr-al-ghazal">Zubayr's Bahr al-Ghazal</a>. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/khedivate-of-egypt">Khedivate of Egypt</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a> in 1874 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/equatoria">Equatoria</a>, and appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-george-gordon">Charles Gordon</a> governor-general to suppress the slave trade.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From 1881 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ahmad">Muhammad Ahmad</a>, led a revolt against Egyptian rule. His forces took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a> in January 1885, killing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-george-gordon">Gordon</a>, and founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mahdist-state">Mahdist State</a>. The Turkiyya left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> with its capital, much of its territory and a lasting legacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/turco-egyptian-sudan.jpg" alt="Turco-Egyptian Sudan" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Abjiklam · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Muhammad_Ali.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1821 CE – 1885 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Khartoum</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Charles Gordon</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Ottoman Turkish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/sudan">Sudan</a>, <a href="/countries/south-sudan">South Sudan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Turkiyya, Egyptian Sudan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1853&amp;focus=turco-egyptian-sudan" aria-label="Turco-Egyptian Sudan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5568 1597 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Turco-Egyptian Sudan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5888 1853L5886 2065M5888 1853L5811 1337M5888 1853L5811 1337M5888 1853L5883 1851M5888 1853L5886 1850M5888 1853L5695 1926"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5886 2065h0M5811 1337h0M5811 1337h0M5883 1851h0M5886 1850h0M5695 1926h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5888 1853h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5888 1853h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Khartoum. <a href="/atlas?year=1853&amp;focus=turco-egyptian-sudan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
